.TH EUCLID-CLI-ESS-GET-SECRET 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-ess-get-secret \- read a secret's value
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli ess get-secret
.BR \-\-name | \-n
.I NAME
[\fB\-\-raw\fR|\fB\-r\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Reads a secret and prints it. This is the only action in euclid that
writes a secret's value out.
.PP
The server records every one of these: who read what, and when. The
value itself is not part of that and never appears in a log line.
.PP
.B \-\-raw
prints the value alone \- no JSON around it, no trailing newline \-
which is what makes it usable from a script:
.PP
.RS
.nf
export PGPASSWORD=$(euclid-cli ess get-secret -n db-password --raw)
.fi
.RE
.PP
Without it the metadata comes too, which is what says how old the value
is, how many times it has been rotated and which key it is encrypted
under.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-name " " \fINAME\fR
Secret name, within the caller's own account and namespace. Required.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-raw
Print the value alone.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The value is printed.
.TP
.B 1
No secret of that name exists, the caller was not granted it, the key
it is encrypted under is gone, or the stored bytes did not decrypt
under the key they name.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-ess (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ess-create-secret (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ess-update-secret (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ess-list-secrets (1)
